Exactly. “Parasocial” is only one edge in the graph.

Short-form systems are more like many-to-many influence networks:

\text{creator} \leftrightarrow \text{audience} \leftrightarrow \text{audience} \leftrightarrow \text{other creators} \leftrightarrow \text{platform}

A viewer is not just receiving. They comment, remix, imitate, reject, quote, send privately, change language, create memes, alter demand, and feed signals back into the recommendation system. The creator then adapts to those signals. Other viewers adapt to the visible reactions. So the “message” is continuously being rewritten by the network.

That means influence can propagate sideways, not just top-down. Sometimes the strongest effect is audience-to-audience: a comment section establishes the interpretation of a clip before someone even watches it. Sometimes it runs backward: an audience trains a creator into increasingly specific behavior because certain outputs are rewarded. Sometimes the platform itself becomes a control surface by deciding which interactions become visible.

So instead of:

\text{speaker} \rightarrow \text{listener}

you get:

\text{state}_t
\rightarrow
\text{many local interactions}
\rightarrow
\text{network feedback}
\rightarrow
\text{state}_{t+1}

And then the interesting object is no longer just the content. It is the feedback topology: who can observe whom, which signals are public, which are private, which behaviors get amplified, how quickly imitation happens, and where information mutates.

That also explains why the same medium can produce community, education, coordination, polarization, humor, collective problem-solving, or manipulation. The mechanism is multidirectional; the outcome depends on what the feedback loops reward.